    "translation": "Commentator: Since the pandemic, we frequently see reports that interest in relocating to rural areas is rising. It is true that the number of relocation consultations has hit record highs, and with the spread of remote work, some people now live in rural areas while keeping their city jobs. Some voices hope this trend will be the trump card that solves rural population decline.\nCommentator: However, looking calmly at the statistics, in the greater Tokyo area, inflows have exceeded outflows in all but a few years, and the number of people moving to rural areas is far too small to offset population decline. There are also reported cases of people whose new life or work did not suit them and who returned to the city within a few years.\nCommentator: Even so, I do not think this movement should be underestimated. What matters is not a tug-of-war over population, but the spread of the so-called 'related population' — people who keep engaging with a particular region while living in the city. The relocation boom is no miracle cure for the population problem, but it should be seen as a change that diversifies how people relate to regions.",
