    "translation": "M: University entrance exams should change to a holistic format that evaluates interviews and extracurricular activities, not just written test scores. W: I'm in the cautious camp. The greatest strength of written exams is their fairness — everyone is measured by the same yardstick. M: But a single test score can't measure persistence or the ability to work with others. W: I admit some things can't be measured. Still, interview scores vary by interviewer, and activity records depend on a family's financial means. M: Those concerns are legitimate. That's exactly why we need a system that publishes evaluation criteria in advance and has multiple interviewers score each candidate. W: Even with such a system, cram schools will commercialize interview prep, and wealthy families will end up advantaged anyway, won't they? M: Written exams already presuppose cram-school prep — it's the same thing. W: That's true. I can't claim written exams are perfectly fair either. M: Then expanding holistic evaluation, at least for part of the admission quota, is the realistic path. W: I'd go the other way. Keep the written exam at the core, and treat interviews and the like not as material that decides pass or fail but merely as supplementary reference. M: If they're merely supplementary, applicants' behavior won't change at all. W: What needs changing is classroom teaching more than the exam. For admissions, fairness comes first — that's my conclusion. M: My position is that holistic evaluation should be expanded step by step, on the condition that the criteria are made public.",
