    "translation": "W: On elementary school homework — I think it should be assigned every day. Without homework, the habit of sitting down to study at home never forms. M: I think uniform homework should be abolished. It's too easy for capable kids and too hard for struggling ones — it serves no one. W: But if you abolish it, won't you just get more children who don't study at home at all? M: There's room for ingenuity there. Make it so each child takes home a task they chose themselves — reading, an independent project, whatever. W: Not every child can choose for themselves. The lower grades need a path laid out for them. M: True, dumping it all on the lower grades won't work. I'll grant that. W: And I'll grant that homework like writing a kanji dozens of times has little meaning. The problem is content, not volume. M: Then, with the content changed, why not leave it to each family whether to do it? W: No — the moment it's optional, the gap widens between families that do it and families that don't. It only works because the school assigns it to everyone. M: I understand that worry about widening gaps. Even so, the bigger loss is children coming to hate studying itself through homework done grudgingly. W: Hence my conclusion: revised-content homework, a small amount daily, for everyone. M: My view is to end uniform homework and switch to choice-based tasks — with a narrowed set of options for the lower grades only.",
