    "translation": "Teacher: Today I'd like to talk about changes in keigo (honorific language). We often hear people say that young people's keigo is deteriorating. It's true that so-called 'part-timer keigo,' like 'yoroshikatta deshou ka,' tends to be criticized as deviating from the norm. But if we look back at the history of the language, keigo has always kept changing. The keigo of the Heian period and today's keigo differ in their very structure. In other words, change itself is nothing abnormal. So what is the essence of the change happening now? I believe the basis of keigo is shifting from social rank to psychological distance. Keigo used to be determined by whether the other person was your superior. Now, however, it is increasingly used according to how close you are to the person — that is, according to a sense of distance. Even the much-criticized part-timer keigo can be seen as a device for maintaining an appropriate distance from customers. So dismissing this as mere 'corruption' means misreading the essence of the change. My conclusion is that keigo is not declining; it is being reorganized on a new principle.",
