    "translation": "Man: Multitasking — handling several tasks at once — is often said to be an essential skill for modern people. Replying to email during meetings, taking calls while preparing documents: it tends to be described as the mark of a capable person. But brain science research is clearly negative about this common belief. The human brain simply cannot process attention-demanding tasks simultaneously. What is actually happening is not parallel processing but rapid switching back and forth between tasks. And every switch wastes time and effort rebuilding concentration. Ironically, there are reports that the people who consider themselves good at multitasking perform worse in experiments. That said, not every combination is harmful. Combinations of tasks that use different kinds of attention — like listening to audio while taking a walk — rarely cause problems. The problem is stacking tasks that compete for the same attention. So what we need is not multitasking training. It is designing an environment where we can focus on one task — turning off notifications, blocking out time, and so on. That is my view.",
