    "translation": "Woman: About guidance signs in stations and airports, people tend to think 'the more information, the kinder.' If someone gets lost, add a sign. If a complaint comes in, put up another notice. As a result, the walls of Japanese stations have ended up plastered with directions, advertisements, and warnings. But the more notices there are, the less people look at the guidance — because the pieces of information drown out each other's voices. What a person who is truly lost needs is not ten pieces of information but the one answer for that moment. That is why I think sign design is the job of deciding not 'what to add' but 'what to subtract.' However, please don't misunderstand: this is not about simply reducing. Cutting things merely to make walls look tidy actually leaves users worse off. The premise of subtraction is observing behavior — where people stop, what they look for. Based on observation, you set priorities and keep only the information most needed at that spot. Kindness is not the quantity of information but the design of priorities. That is my conclusion from years of working on guidance signage.",
